Named-Project Integration Exception

Notwithstanding the restrictions above, the Logos Project grants the Cipher
project and the Esoptron protocol — the foundational protocol underlying
Eidolon — (together, the "Authorized Projects") a limited, worldwide,
royalty-free, non-exclusive, non-transferable license to use, link against,
integrate, and redistribute the Eidolon Public Interface — defined as (a) the
publicly published API surface and integration contracts of the Software, and
(b) the compiled `eidolon_crypto` native distribution (binary wheel) — solely as
a dependency of those projects, including within distributions released under
those projects' MIT license, provided that:

  1. Attribution. Each Authorized Project, and any redistribution of it, must
     retain a visible credit to "Eidolon — Logos Project" with a link to the
     canonical Eidolon repository in its documentation or credits surface.

  2. No source reuse or reproduction. This exception conveys no right to the
     proprietary source code, the Reserved Components, or any cryptographic
     internals, and no right to reverse engineer, decompile, reconstruct, or
     create a derivative of them. It covers integration of the published
     interface and the compiled artifact only; the compiled artifact may not be
     disassembled or otherwise used to recover the underlying source.

  3. Scope. The exception extends only to the Authorized Projects named above.
     It does not transfer to forks, third parties, or other works, and does not
     permit removing or relicensing the proprietary components themselves.

  4. MIT compatibility. Within the Authorized Projects, the MIT-licensed portions
     remain MIT; this exception governs only the Eidolon-originated material they
     incorporate, which remains the property of the Logos Project.

